@startuml ProximaDB WAL State Machine
!theme plain
title ProximaDB - WAL Segment State Machine Diagram

[*] --> Created : new()

state Created
Created : Empty segment
Created : No data written

state Active
Active : Accepting writes
Active : In-memory buffer
Active : Not yet synced

state Syncing
Syncing : Writing to disk
Syncing : Buffer locked
Syncing : No new writes

state Synced
Synced : Persisted to disk
Synced : Available for flush
Synced : Read-only

state Flushing
Flushing : Being processed
Flushing : Copying to storage
Flushing : Cannot be modified

state Flushed
Flushed : Data in storage
Flushed : Can be released
Flushed : Marked for cleanup

state Archived
Archived : Moved to cold storage
Archived : Metadata retained
Archived : Data compressed

state Released
Released : Memory freed
Released : File deleted
Released : No longer accessible

Created --> Active : first write
Active --> Active : append data
Active --> Syncing : sync triggered

Syncing --> Synced : sync complete
Syncing --> Active : sync failed\n[retry]

Synced --> Flushing : flush initiated
Synced --> Archived : archive policy\n[old segments]

Flushing --> Flushed : flush success
Flushing --> Synced : flush failed\n[retry]

Flushed --> Released : cleanup cycle
Flushed --> Archived : retention policy

Archived --> Released : retention expired

Released --> [*]

note right of Active
  Triggers for sync:
  - Size threshold
  - Time interval
  - Explicit flush
  - Memory pressure
end note

note left of Flushing
  Flush process:
  1. Deserialize records
  2. Route to storage engine
  3. Update metadata
  4. Mark complete
end note

note bottom of Archived
  Archive conditions:
  - Age > threshold
  - Access frequency low
  - Backup requirement
end note

@enduml